{
  "requirement": "The `requirement` string in each evidence file MUST match all Markdown inside its fenced `{req}` directive, excluding leading and trailing whitespace. Habeas MUST report changed requirement text.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The implementation and tests satisfy `requirements.ci.requirement_text`.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `validate_evidence_file` in `src/evidence.rs` is marked `{impl}` at line 466. It reads the `requirement` field (or `example` for examples) and compares it with the declared text. On a mismatch it pushes an error of the form `\"<label>: requirement text changed at <source>\"` followed by `compact_diff` output.\n- The declared text comes from `collect_definitions`, which takes `content[block.body_start..block.body_end].trim()`. That is everything between the opening and closing `:::` fences, with leading and trailing whitespace removed, as the requirement states. That line is marked `{impl}` for `requirements.ci.span`.\n- `definition_blocks` finds the body span. It ignores fences inside code blocks and honours fence lengths. Markdown that contains nested code fences, or literal `{req}` text, is therefore kept in full.\n- A changed text surfaces as an entry in the report's `errors`, so the check is reported.\n- The check is strict equality against the trimmed declaration, so evidence text with surrounding whitespace would be flagged. I read this as consistent with the requirement.\n- The \"must not be empty\" check sits under the same marker. It is a small, closely related extra.\n\n**Tests**\n- `validates_full_names_requirement_text_and_input_lines` (`{test}` at line 1366) uses a requirement body that has surrounding newlines and an embedded fenced code block containing `{req}`. The evidence text is the trimmed body, and the test asserts no errors.\n- `collects_missing_orphan_format_false_and_changed_line_errors` (`{test}` at line 1410) stores `\"Old text.\"` for a requirement that now says `First.`. It asserts that the output contains `requirement text changed`, `- Old text.` and `+ First.`.\n- The same test covers a non-string `requirement` (`requirement must be a string`) and an empty requirement body (`requirement requirements.empty has no text`).\n\nThe matching and mismatch cases are both covered, and I found no unmarked code that implements part of this requirement.",
